<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amsterdam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amsterdam is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and was the commercial and financial heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> in the seventeenth century. It hosted the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>'s largest chamber, the Amsterdam Exchange Bank and a major stock market. The city's wealth and institutions underpinned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amsterdam.jpg" alt="Amsterdam" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrés Barrios · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imagen_de_los_canales_conc%C3%A9ntricos_en_%C3%81msterdam.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Amsterdam lies in the province of North Holland, in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, at an elevation of about 2 metres below sea level. The river Amstel ends in the city centre and connects to a large number of canals that eventually terminate in the IJ. The surrounding land is flat and formed of large polders, and the city is connected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea-canal">North Sea Canal</a>. More than 100 kilometres of canals run through it, most of them navigable. The three main ones are the Prinsengracht, the Herengracht and the Keizersgracht.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Farmers settled around the area of the later city about three millennia ago, along the prehistoric IJ and upstream on its tributary the Amstel. A permanent settlement was not possible until water issues were controlled, because the river mouth and banks were too wet. Amsterdam was founded at the mouth of the Amstel, which was dammed to control flooding, and it began as a small fishing village in the 12th century. Its population was around 1,000 in 1300. It grew in the 15th and 16th centuries, mainly through the Baltic grain trade after the Burgundian victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-hanseatic-war">Dutch–Hanseatic War</a> in 1441.</p><p>The population then rose sharply during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a>. Many people from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-netherlands">Southern Netherlands</a> fled north, especially after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> fell to Spanish forces in 1585, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, Germans and Scandinavians also settled there. The population more than doubled between 1585 and 1610, stood at about 50,000 in 1600 and reached 200,000 in the 1660s. It then stabilised around 240,000 for most of the 18th century. The Amsterdam Stock Exchange was founded in 1602. In the 19th and 20th centuries the city expanded, and new neighbourhoods and suburbs were built.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Amsterdam is the capital and largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, though it is not the seat of government, which is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>. Its 17th-century canal belt, the grachten, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> and preserves narrow canal houses with gabled façades. The Rijksmuseum, opened in 1885, holds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-golden-age">Dutch Golden Age</a> art, and the Stedelijk Museum opened in 1895. The city has two universities, the University of Amsterdam and the Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam, and hosted the Summer Olympics in 1928.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amsterdam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sixteenth-century Antwerp was the first great hub of a world economy linking the Baltic, England, southern Germany, the Mediterranean and the Iberian empires, and its commercial techniques passed to <b>Amsterdam</b> after 1585 along with many of its merchants and craftsmen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>This allowed the company to finance much of its Asian business without constant transfers of bullion from <b>Amsterdam</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Amsterdam</b> became the staple market of European trade and finance, with the Bank of Amsterdam (1609) and an exchange where company shares were bought and sold.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch merchants, with larger ships, cheaper freight and a stronger financial base in <b>Amsterdam</b>, took over much of the Baltic grain and timber trade passing through the Danish Sound, while the herring shoals moved from the Baltic to the North Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The country’s first railway, between <b>Amsterdam</b> and Haarlem, had opened in 1839, symbolising a slow turn towards modernisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>New Amsterdam, outside Fort <b>Amsterdam</b>, became a hub for trade between North America, the Caribbean and Europe, loading pelts, lumber and tobacco.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
